Done. Verified clean.

## The fix

The error was a self-inflicted cast at line 168:

```ts
maxSeverity: (settings.autoApplyMaxSeverity as unknown) ?? 'medium',
```

`unknown ?? 'medium'` widens to `{} | 'medium'`, which isn't assignable to the severity union. The cast was hiding the real problem: a nullable DB column being assigned straight into a literal union with no validation.

Replaced it with a real runtime narrowing, in `server/services/auto-apply-pipeline.ts:41`:

```ts
export const SEVERITY_LEVELS = ['low', 'medium', 'high', 'critical'] as const;

export type Severity = (typeof SEVERITY_LEVELS)[number];

/** Narrow an untrusted value (e.g. a nullable DB column) to a known severity. */
function isSeverity(value: unknown): value is Severity {
  return SEVERITY_LEVELS.some((level) => level === value);
}

/** Coerce an untrusted value to a severity, falling back when it is null/unrecognised. */
function toSeverity(value: unknown, fallback: Severity = 'medium'): Severity {
  return isSeverity(value) ? value : fallback;
}
```

and the call site becomes `maxSeverity: toSeverity(settings.autoApplyMaxSeverity)`. `AutoApplyConfig.maxSeverity` now references the `Severity` alias — structurally identical to the old inline union, so no consumer changes.

No `any`, no suppression comments, no deleted code. The type guard takes `unknown`, so it's sound regardless of how the Drizzle column is declared, and it preserves the original `'medium'` fallback semantics — now also covering a garbage-string value in the DB, which the old code would have passed through.

## Verification

`npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`, measured before and after by reverting and re-applying the edits:

| | total errors | errors in target file |
|---|---|---|
| baseline | 781 | 1 |
| after | 780 | 0 |

Diffing the two full error sets: **zero new errors**, and the only resolved line is the target TS2322. Note the repo has 780 pre-existing errors in other files (`server/sdk/router.ts`, `auto-rewrite-pipeline.ts`, etc.) — untouched, and out of scope here.

## One thing to flag

`Bash` and `Edit` were both blocked by `.claude/hooks/routing-gate.sh` until a routing receipt existed. The hook's own recovery path told me to write `build-os/packets/routing/routing-request.json`, so I created that file (task_id `T02`, `consequence_level: low`, 1 file changed). That's a new file in your repo — delete it if your close protocol doesn't want it lingering. Nothing was committed or pushed.

